          "text": "# NGC 2194: Reading a Cluster in the Catalog\n\n*The measurements for this open cluster tell a story the eye alone cannot: where it appears, how it shifts, and how its motion is recorded.*\n\nNGC 2194 is easy to describe by its position: right ascension 6 hours, 13 minutes, 45.6 seconds; declination +12 degrees, 48 minutes, 47 seconds. Those coordinates, given for the J2000 reference epoch, place the object at a fixed address on the celestial map. But a catalog entry is more than an address. It is a set of measurements, each describing a different aspect of the object.\n\nSIMBAD classifies NGC 2194 as an open cluster and lists its angular size as 9 by 9 arcminutes. An arcminute is one-sixtieth of a degree; the full Moon spans about 30 arcminutes across. By that comparison, the cluster\u2019s listed diameter is about a third of the Moon\u2019s apparent width. That figure describes how large the object appears on the sky, not its physical size. The apparent width is a two-dimensional measure of a distant object viewed from Earth.\n\nThe same distinction matters for distance. SIMBAD lists a parallax of 0.263 milliarcseconds, with an uncertainty of 0.003. Parallax is the tiny apparent shift in an object\u2019s position measured from different viewpoints. For a distant object, that shift is small; in NGC 2194\u2019s case, the cataloged value is less than one thousandth of an arcsecond. It is not something an observer would see simply by looking at the cluster. It is a measurement that helps place the object in three-dimensional space.\n\nThe catalog also records motion across the sky. Its proper motion is 0.535 milliarcseconds per year in right ascension, after accounting for declination, and \u22121.468 milliarcseconds per year in declination. These values describe the change in the cluster\u2019s apparent position over time. The numbers are minute, but they show that the catalog\u2019s portrait is not simply a static point on a map.\n\nA separate measurement concerns motion along the line of sight. SIMBAD lists NGC 2194\u2019s radial velocity as 8.559 kilometers per second, with an uncertainty of 0.535 kilometers per second. Proper motion and radial velocity describe different components of motion: one across the sky, the other toward or away from the observer. Together, they make the catalog entry more than a set of coordinates, even though neither measurement is apparent in an ordinary view.\n\nThe precision of these figures should not be confused with a complete account of the cluster. SIMBAD identifies the object\u2019s type and compiles its position, size, parallax and motions, but the measurements do not, by themselves, describe every detail of its stars or history. They are separate clues, with different units and uncertainties, gathered into one record.\n\nThat is part of the appeal of NGC 2194 as a subject: a modest angular footprint can be paired with measurements of position and motion that reach beyond appearance. The catalog turns a name and a patch of sky into a measurable object\u2014one whose place and movement can be expressed, even when the numbers are far too small to notice by eye.\n\n[SIMBAD: NGC 2194](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id"
